course: myths
lesson: 2
title: Your amazing brain and senses
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Dramatic music, a glowing brain on screen, a YouTuber whispering: "You only use TEN PERCENT of your brain. Imagine unlocking the other ninety!" It feels like a secret superpower waiting inside your head.
explain: Brain myths are thrilling: a tongue with neat taste zones, people born "right-brained" or "not maths people", Mozart making babies brainy, eyeballs popping out if you sneeze with them open, French learned from tapes under your pillow.
explain: The real brain is even more amazing: scans show you use virtually all of it, different parts busy at different moments, and it keeps building new connections all your life, so practice really changes it.
explain: Close your eyes and touch your nose: no sight, hearing, smell, taste or touch told you where your hand was, so you have more than five senses.
explain: When a friend believes a brain myth, ask "who was studied, for how long?" and "if this were true, what else would happen?"; and notice when the person telling you is selling a fix.
fact: Brain scans show people use virtually all of their brain, though not every part at the same moment; damage to almost any area causes problems.
fact: The "ten percent" myth spread through misquoted scientists, self-help books and films such as Lucy.
fact: The list of exactly five senses comes from Aristotle over two thousand years ago; scientists now count many more, such as balance, sensing where your limbs are, temperature, pain and hunger.
fact: The tongue map with sweet at the tip and bitter at the back came from a 1901 German study that was mistranslated; every taste can be sensed wherever there are taste buds.
fact: The two halves of the brain work together for almost everything; nobody is a "left-brained" or "right-brained" type of person.
fact: The "Mozart effect" came from a small 1993 study of students that found a short boost on one kind of puzzle; later studies found no lasting cleverness from listening to Mozart.
fact: Closing your eyes when you sneeze is a reflex; your eyes would not pop out, because muscles hold them and the sneeze's pressure does not push them out.
fact: For most of the 20th century scientists thought adults never grow new brain cells; now we know some new cells form in a few areas, and the brain makes new connections all life long.
fact: Practice and good teaching change how well people do in maths; there are no born "non-maths people".
fact: People often dream of falling or dying and wake up fine; a dream cannot hurt your body.
fact: You cannot learn new words from tapes while deeply asleep, but sleep does help you keep what you learned while awake.
fact: When someone sells a cure for a problem, first check the problem is real.
exercise: How much of your brain do you really use? | Virtually all of it, different parts at different times | Exactly ten percent, while the rest of it is fast asleep | About half, the left side only | Only the front part, the rest is spare
exercise: If you only used ten percent of your brain, what would be strange about head injuries? | Most injuries would cause no problems at all | Every single injury to the brain would make you a genius | Injuries would only ever hurt on the left side of your body | Injuries to the brain would all heal in a single day
exercise: Who first listed exactly five senses? | Aristotle, over two thousand years ago | A doctor in the 1990s | Isaac Newton, just after the apple fell on his head | A school textbook in 1950
exercise: Which of these is a real sense that is not in the old list of five? | Balance | Luck | Memory | Shyness
exercise: Where on your tongue can you taste sweet things? | Anywhere there are taste buds | Only at the very tip of the tongue | Only right at the back of the tongue | Only along the two sides of the tongue
exercise: Where did the tongue map come from? | A 1901 German study that was mistranslated | A careful modern brain scan at a big hospital | Ancient Egyptian cooks | A sweet company's advert
exercise: Cousin Ella says she is "a right-brain person". What does science say? | Both halves work together for almost everything | The right half does all art and nothing else | Only boys are left-brained and girls right-brained | You can switch halves by sleeping on one side
exercise: What did the 1993 "Mozart effect" study actually test? | Students on one puzzle, for a short time | Babies who listened to Mozart every day for ten years | Dogs learning tricks with music | Old people learning to play piano
exercise: Why do most people close their eyes when they sneeze? | It is a reflex the body does by itself | To stop their eyeballs from popping out | Because light makes the sneeze louder | Because the nose pulls the eyelids down
exercise: Nan says dead brain cells never come back. What have scientists found since? | Some new cells form in adults, and new connections grow all life | Brain cells never die at all, even in old age | Every brain cell is replaced each year | Brains stop changing at age ten
exercise: Kai says he is "just not a maths person". What is truer? | Practice and good teaching make people better at maths | Maths skill is fixed forever on the day that you are born | Only people with big heads can do maths | Maths skill comes from eating the right fish
exercise: A friend says if you die in a dream you die in real life. What proves it false? | People who had such dreams are alive to tell them | Nobody in the whole world has ever dreamed about falling | Dreams like that only ever happen to grown-ups, not children | Doctors wake everybody up just before the bad part comes
exercise: What does sleep really do for learning? | It helps you keep what you learned while awake | It teaches you new words from tapes | It deletes everything you learned that day to make room for more | It has nothing to do with learning at all
exercise: A YouTuber sells a course to "unlock the other 90%". What is the wise first question? | Is the problem even real? | How much does the course cost? | Which colour is the brain in the advert? | How many people liked the video?
exercise: Your quiz card says humans have exactly five senses. What is a friendly way to argue? | "I read there are more, like balance! Could we add a bonus question?" | "This card is rubbish and so is your quiz." | "You're wrong, there are exactly five hundred." | Say nothing, because quiz cards are always right
exercise: Which simple experiment tests the tongue map? | Dab sugar water on the back and salty water on the tip | Eat a whole lemon with your eyes shut | Hold your nose and sing a song | Count the bumps on your tongue in a mirror to find each taste zone
test: "nah mate ur eyes literally fly out if u sneeze with em open, my bro saw it" What's true? | Closing them is a reflex; nobody's eyes pop out | He's right, it happens about once a week | Only blue eyes pop out, brown ones don't | They pop out for a moment but go straight back in again
test: You put sugar water on the back of your tongue. What happens? | You taste sweet | You taste nothing at all | You taste only bitter | You taste salt instead
test: In a film, a pill lets the hero "use 100% of her brain" and move things with her mind. What's wrong with the idea? | We already use virtually all of our brain | Nothing, a pill like that could really do it | It should say half of your brain, not one hundred percent | Only grown-up men are able to use their whole brain
test: Close your eyes and touch your nose. Which sense told you where your hand was? | The sense of where your limbs are | Hearing | Smell | Taste
test: An advert on a baby toy box says Mozart makes babies cleverer. Why is that weak? | The study was on grown-up students for a short time | The study was on babies and found it worked | Mozart's music is too fast for babies to hear | The advert is from a doctor, so what it says must all be true
test: "are you thick or what, some ppl just can't do maths, end of" Kai's mate says. What do you reply? | Brains get stronger with practice; let's practise together | You're right, Kai should give up maths now | Only right-brained people can't do maths, so Kai should stop | Maths skill depends on your star sign
test: Hassan's cousin plays French tapes all night. What would happen if Hassan tested his French after a week? | He'd know little new; learning awake before sleep works better | He'd speak fluent French by Friday | He'd forget English instead | He'd only dream in French but speak it perfectly
test: Nan learned at school that brain cells never grow back. Why might her book say that? | Scientists thought so then; newer research changed the picture | Her teacher made it up as a joke | Brain cells really did stop growing in people back in those days | Old books are always right and new ones wrong
test: Ella failed an online quiz and now says "I'm right-brained so I'm rubbish at maths". What is the risk of this label? | It can stop her trying to get better | It will make her better at art instead of maths | It actually changes the shape of her brain for ever | It has no effect at all on what she does or tries
test: Which tells you a brain claim might be a sales trick? | The person telling you is selling the fix for it | It comes from a large, careful study with thousands of people | It was checked by many scientists | It matches what brain scans show
test: Your friend at a sleepover with a torch says "die in a dream, die for real". What's a kind way to answer? | "My dad had that dream and he's fine! Happy stories now?" | "That's stupid and you're stupid for saying it." | "Quick, nobody fall asleep tonight!" | "Only if the dream is in colour."
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the utilisation of the human brain? | Virtually the entirety of the brain is employed, its regions active at differing times | Merely ten percent of the brain is employed at any time | One hemisphere remains wholly inactive throughout life | The brain ceases all activity during sleep
exam: Identify the origin of the doctrine that the human being possesses precisely five senses. | An enumeration made by Aristotle more than two millennia ago | A census conducted by physicians in the present century across all nations | A determination derived from modern brain scanning | A regulation issued by an examination board
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the perception of taste upon the tongue. | Each taste may be perceived wherever taste buds are present | Sweetness is perceived exclusively at the tip, bitterness at the rear | Bitterness is perceived exclusively at the sides | Saltiness is perceived exclusively at the rear
exam: It is given that an individual attends to recordings in a foreign language whilst in deep sleep. Ascertain the outcome. | No new vocabulary is acquired, sleep assisting only material learned whilst awake | Fluency in the language is attained within one week | The native language of the individual is forfeited | Vocabulary is acquired at twice the waking rate
